#
# For a description of the syntax of this configuration file,
# see kconfig-language at https://www.kernel.org/doc/Documentation/kbuild/kconfig-language.txt
#
# Derived from Apache NuttX mm/kasan/Kconfig at commit b4aa94d. The software
# tag, hardware tag and global data options of the original are not offered:
# the tag modes need ARMv8 top byte ignore or ARMv8.5 MTE, which no TizenRT
# target provides, and global data checking needs a two pass link and linker
# script support which is not in place yet.
#

config MM_KASAN
	bool "Kernel Address Sanitizer"
	default n
	---help---
		KASan is a compiler assisted tool for detecting memory errors:
		heap buffer overflow, heap buffer underflow and use after free.
		Every instrumented load and store is checked against a shadow map
		maintained by the memory manager, so an error is reported at the
		instruction which causes it rather than at the next allocator
		operation.

		The shadow map holds one bit per machine word and is carved out of
		the tail of each heap region, costing about 3 percent of the RAM it
		covers. Expect the image to grow by 30 to 50 percent and to run
		two to three times slower.

		This is a debug and CI option. Never enable it in a production
		image.

		NOTE: enabling this option alone builds the runtime but does not
		yet instrument anything. The compiler flags are added per board;
		see MM_KASAN_INSTRUMENT_ALL.

config MM_KASAN_INSTRUMENT
	bool

if MM_KASAN

choice
	prompt "KASan mode"
	default MM_KASAN_GENERIC

config MM_KASAN_NONE
	bool "Instrument but do not check"
	select MM_KASAN_INSTRUMENT
	---help---
		Emit and call the instrumentation entry points, but never report.
		Useful only for measuring what the call overhead alone costs,
		separately from the shadow lookup.

config MM_KASAN_GENERIC
	bool "Generic mode"
	select MM_KASAN_INSTRUMENT
	---help---
		Software shadow memory. Requires no hardware support.

endchoice

config MM_KASAN_INSTRUMENT_ALL
	bool "Instrument the entire image"
	depends on MM_KASAN_INSTRUMENT
	default y
	---help---
		Add -fsanitize=kernel-address to the flags for the whole image.
		The memory manager itself is always excluded; see os/mm/kasan/Make.defs.

		If this is disabled, instrumentation must be requested per
		directory or per file instead.

		This port targets the flat build, where there is one image and
		one heap, so the whole image is instrumented.

		NOTE: the board Make.defs must act on this symbol for it to have
		any effect. A board which does not yet do so builds unchanged.

config MM_KASAN_INSTRUMENT_BOARD
	bool "Instrument the vendor board support code as well"
	depends on MM_KASAN_INSTRUMENT_ALL
	default n
	---help---
		Include the vendor board support code, os/board/<board>/src, in
		instrumentation.

		Off by default. That code is large, the flat image has a fixed
		partition to fit into, and it is the least likely place for a
		first bring-up to want coverage. Bring the board up with
		TizenRT's own code instrumented, confirm a clean boot with no
		reports, then turn this on to widen.

		On rtl8721csm this also keeps the sanitizer away from the
		TrustZone secure gateway veneers which the prebuilt cmse_implib
		archives call into.

		Note that the vendor libraries which ship as binaries cannot be
		instrumented at all whatever this is set to, so some of the board
		remains invisible either way.

if MM_KASAN_INSTRUMENT

config MM_KASAN_REGIONS
	int "Number of regions KASan can track"
	default 8
	---help---
		Maximum number of memory regions that can be registered. This must
		be at least KMM_REGIONS multiplied by KMM_NHEAPS, counting every
		heap that registers with KASan.

		Registration of a region beyond this limit is refused with a
		message and that region is left unchecked.

config MM_KASAN_SELFTEST
	bool "Self test"
	default n
	---help---
		Build kernel/debug/kasan_selftest.c, which performs one deliberate
		memory error against the kernel heap on request, and the
		kasan_test application which drives it.

		A system with no memory errors and a system where instrumentation
		was never emitted are both silent, so a clean boot on its own says
		nothing about whether KASan is working. This is what tells them
		apart. Enable it for bring up and for a CI job, and turn it off
		once the port is trusted on a board.

		Every case but the in bounds one is expected to report and to
		assert. That is the pass condition. Do not enable this on a
		product image.

		Requires BUILTIN_APPS so the application registers as a TASH
		command.

config MM_KASAN_LOCK_WORDS
	int "Shadow words to write per interrupt-disabled section"
	default 256
	---help---
		How many shadow words kasan_poison() and kasan_unpoison() write
		before releasing the lock and taking it again, so that interrupts
		are serviced during a long range.

		This matters at registration, which poisons a whole region in one
		call. A region of a few megabytes is tens of thousands of shadow
		words, and writing them in one unbroken interrupt-disabled section
		stalls the system for long enough to starve a driver or trip a
		watchdog, every boot. Slow memory such as PSRAM makes it worse.

		Lower it if boot still stalls, raise it if registration is
		noticeably slow. The default writes one kilobyte of shadow per
		section on a 32 bit target, which covers 128 KB of heap.

		Each individual word is still updated under the lock, so no
		read-modify-write is ever torn. What is given up is only that a
		whole range stops being updated atomically, which no caller needs:
		at registration nothing has been allocated from the region yet,
		and on malloc and free the block is not reachable by anything else
		while its shadow is being changed.

config MM_KASAN_START_MANUAL
	bool "Leave checking off until it is asked for"
	default n
	---help---
		Register the regions and maintain the shadow map as usual, but do
		not begin reporting until something calls kasan_start().

		Without this, checking goes live the moment the first heap
		registers, which is very early in os_start(). The whole of boot
		then runs instrumented: drivers, Wi-Fi, the filesystem and the
		binary manager. That is what you want eventually, but it is not
		where to begin, because a report from any of them stops the board
		before it reaches a prompt.

		With this set the board boots silently and you arm KASan when you
		are ready, so a report can only come from the code you are
		testing.

		The shadow map is still maintained while disarmed. Every
		allocation and free keeps it accurate, so when you do arm, blocks
		handed out during boot are tracked correctly and a later use
		after free of one of them is still caught.

		What is lost is anything that went wrong while disarmed: KASan
		reports at the moment of access, never retroactively. Once your
		own tests are clean, boot once with this off, because the boot
		path is where driver and vendor bugs surface.

		The kasan_test application arms and disarms it from the shell.

config MM_KASAN_DISABLE_NULL_POINTER_CHECK
	bool "Do not check for NULL pointer access"
	default n
	---help---
		By default a load or store through a NULL pointer is reported even
		when it falls outside any registered region. Disable if some driver
		legitimately maps a page at address zero.

config MM_KASAN_DISABLE_READS_CHECK
	bool "Do not instrument reads"
	default n
	---help---
		Instrument only writes. Roughly halves the overhead, at the cost of
		missing out of bounds reads. Most corruption bugs are writes, so
		this is a reasonable first configuration on a slow target.

config MM_KASAN_DISABLE_WRITES_CHECK
	bool "Do not instrument writes"
	default n
	---help---
		Instrument only reads. Rarely what you want; see
		MM_KASAN_DISABLE_READS_CHECK.

config MM_KASAN_DISABLE_READ_PANIC
	bool "Do not panic on an invalid read"
	default n
	---help---
		Report the error and carry on rather than asserting. The report is
		less useful because the crash dump is not taken at the faulting
		instruction, but the system keeps running, which helps when
		collecting several errors in one boot.

config MM_KASAN_DISABLE_WRITE_PANIC
	bool "Do not panic on an invalid write"
	default n
	---help---
		As MM_KASAN_DISABLE_READ_PANIC, for writes. Note that execution
		continues over already corrupted memory, so later failures may be
		misleading.

endif # MM_KASAN_INSTRUMENT
endif # MM_KASAN
